# R23 A Place to Sleep (side quest, escort): the sick, the children and the old folk to the Silent Church. By hand, polished by Gemini, unvoiced.

~ morgan
morgan [weary]: No song reaches the Silent Church, they say. Sleep under those eaves, and you wake as yourself. My memory is mostly mist now, yet I know fire takes the bedridden first. Help me move them.
set wv_sleep_asked
quest a_place_to_sleep asked
-> morgan_menu

~ morgan_back
morgan [neutral]: How does it stand? The fever wagon, the little ones, the elders. I cannot set my bottles down until they are behind that stone.
-> morgan_menu

~ morgan_menu
* [if flag.sleep_count >= 3] Everyone is inside the church. -> morgan_done
* Who still needs moving? -> morgan_need
* [ends:a_place_to_sleep if flag.sleep_count < 3] We cannot move them in time. -> morgan_fail
* Step away -> END

~ morgan_need
morgan [weary]: Eight sick folk in the cart, the children huddled under the inn awning, and the elders planted on their kitchen chairs. Each has a stubborn reason to stay. I know fever, not stubbornness.
-> morgan_menu

~ morgan_done
morgan [smile]: My chest feels lighter, though I could not tell you whose habit it is to hope. Thank you. They will have a quiet night.
quest a_place_to_sleep sheltered
set sleep_resolved
heal 3d8 party
-> END

~ morgan_fail
morgan [sad]: Then I will stay by the cart with whatever draughts I have left. Go on. The smoke on the hill will not wait for us.
quest a_place_to_sleep scattered
set sleep_resolved
-> END

~ sick
Narrator: Eight fevered villagers lie packed in straw and wool blankets, an old hound curled across their boots. Every rut in the cobbles draws a thin moan from under the canvas.
* [Athletics DC 13] Haul the wagon smoothly over the ruts -> sick_ok | sick_slow
* [Medicine DC 12] Brace their limbs and ease them along -> sick_ok | sick_slow
* Carry them across by stretcher, one by one -> sick_carry

~ sick_ok
Narrator: You lean into the shafts and roll the cart straight to the porch in one smooth pull, lifting each patient over the threshold.
set sleep_sick
set sleep_count += 1
-> END

~ sick_slow
Narrator: An iron rim drops into a gutter and someone gasps under the blankets. You chock the wheel, let them catch their breath, and look for a gentler line.
-> sick

~ sick_carry
Narrator: You spend a damp hour shuttling canvas litters through the dark. No one makes a sound, and at the end of it your coats smell of camphor and everyone is under stone.
time +60
set sleep_sick
set sleep_count += 1
-> END

~ children
Narrator: A tangle of children huddles beneath the dripping inn awning, coats buttoned to their chins. The eldest boy has been told this is a game of hide-and-seek, and his glare says he knows better.
* [Performance DC 12] Spin a story all the way to the church -> children_ok | children_slow
* [Persuasion DC 12] Promise they can meet the church's stone knight -> children_ok | children_slow
* Carry them over in pairs and bundles -> children_carry

~ children_ok
Narrator: They trail after your hem like goslings in the dark, trading whispers about the next verse. On the church steps the littlest tugs your cuff and asks, "What happens next?"
set sleep_children
set sleep_count += 1
-> END

~ children_slow
Narrator: One chin wobbles, then a second, and a thin wail starts up under the eaves. You drop to one knee on the wet stones to try another tack.
-> children

~ children_carry
Narrator: It takes four journeys with small boots kicking your ribs. Nobody cries, though the smallest one leaves her teeth marks in your leather bracer.
time +20
set sleep_children
set sleep_count += 1
-> END

~ old
Narrator: A line of elders sits planted on kitchen chairs against the inn wall, shawls tucked tight around their knees. One cradles a tin thermos of broth; her neighbour holds a fowling piece across his lap.
* [Persuasion DC 14] Insist the church needs good chairs more than the Round -> old_ok | old_stuck
* [Insight DC 13] Spot each small comfort they refuse to leave behind -> old_ok | old_stuck
* Lift them chair and all, one at a time -> old_carry

~ old_ok
Narrator: Up they get, muttering at their knees, and shuffle off with their chairs like mourners at a parish vestry meeting. The man with the fowling piece leaves his shells in his pocket.
set sleep_old
set sleep_count += 1
-> END

~ old_stuck
Narrator: Thin arms cross over wool shawls. The woman with the tin thermos unscrews the cap and pours herself a steaming tot. You will need a sharper angle.
-> old

~ old_carry
Narrator: You hoist them up, oak legs and wool blankets together, while they critique your footing the entire way. At the porch, a widow squints at the stone knight and remarks that he has her late husband's jaw, which does not sound forgiving.
time +20
set sleep_old
set sleep_count += 1
-> END
